<h1>Biblical History: From the Ancient Near East to the Formation of Israel</h1>

<p>To understand the Bible as a historical document, we must first understand the world in which it was written and the events it describes. The stories of Genesis, Exodus, and the formation of Israel did not occur in a vacuum — they unfolded within the rich and complex civilizations of the <strong>Ancient Near East</strong>.</p>

<h2>Part 1: The Ancient Near East — The Stage of Biblical History</h2>

<h3>Mesopotamia</h3>
<p><strong>Mesopotamia</strong> (Greek: "land between the rivers") refers to the region between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, in present-day Iraq. It is one of the cradles of human civilization, home to some of the world's earliest cities, writing systems, and legal codes.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Sumer</strong> (c. 4500–1900 BC): The earliest civilisation in Mesopotamia. The Sumerians invented cuneiform writing (around 3200 BC), one of the first writing systems in human history. Cities like Ur and Eridu were centres of Sumerian culture. The biblical city of Ur — birthplace of Abraham — was a Sumerian city.</li>
  <li><strong>Akkad and Babylon</strong>: Sargon of Akkad (c. 2334–2279 BC) built the world's first empire. Later, Babylon became the dominant power in Mesopotamia, and its legal tradition (e.g., the Code of Hammurabi, c. 1754 BC) influenced ancient Near Eastern societies widely.</li>
  <li><strong>Assyria</strong>: A powerful empire in northern Mesopotamia, the Assyrians eventually conquered the Northern Kingdom of Israel in 722 BC.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Egypt</h3>
<p>Egypt's civilization arose along the Nile River valley in northeastern Africa. The pharaohs ruled a highly organised society with advanced agriculture, architecture (the pyramids), and religion. Egypt features prominently in biblical history: Abraham visited Egypt (Genesis 12), Joseph was sold into slavery there (Genesis 37), and the entire nation of Israel lived as slaves in Egypt before the Exodus (Exodus 1–15).</p>

<h3>Canaan</h3>
<p><strong>Canaan</strong> refers roughly to the region of present-day Israel, Palestine, Lebanon, and parts of Syria and Jordan. It was a crossroads between Egypt to the south and Mesopotamia to the northeast. Various city-states (including Jericho, Jerusalem, Hebron, and Shechem) dotted the landscape. God promised this land to Abraham and his descendants (Genesis 12:7), making it the central theatre of Israel's history.</p>

<h2>Part 2: Biblical Timeline</h2>

